WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.204 --> 00:00:04.620
SANDY JONES:
Afternoon, everybody,

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:06.830
and welcome to Cloud
OnAir live webinar

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:08.510
series from Google Cloud.

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:10.680
We're hosting a live webinar
series every Tuesday.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:12.930
So you can check the agenda
and see a topic that's

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:14.510
useful for you and join.

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:16.100
My name is Sandy
Jones, and today I'm

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.183
going to be walking you
through Engaging Meetings,

00:00:18.183 --> 00:00:20.194
making them easy,
accessible, and everywhere.

00:00:20.194 --> 00:00:21.860
Throughout the session,
you can actually

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:23.615
ask questions using
the platform below.

00:00:23.615 --> 00:00:25.760
We've got some people
ready and waiting

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:26.930
to answer your questions.

00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:28.596
And at the very end,
I'm going to choose

00:00:28.596 --> 00:00:31.260
a few to just do in a Q&amp;A
session and answer live.

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:32.704
So let's get started.

00:00:32.704 --> 00:00:34.370
Like I said, I want
to talk to you today

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:35.660
about meeting experiences.

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:37.910
And you know, at Google,
Video Conferencing

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:40.545
is a crucial tool for
how we do business.

00:00:40.545 --> 00:00:42.170
If you walk the halls
in Google, you'll

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:45.430
see every meeting room, no
matter how small or large,

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:48.290
there's faces in the room,
but also faces on the screen.

00:00:48.290 --> 00:00:50.810
And we produce, I think, 10
years of video conference

00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:51.576
every single day.

00:00:51.576 --> 00:00:53.450
And it's become a valuable
tool, because it's

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:56.630
allowed us to build our
culture, be more agile, and more

00:00:56.630 --> 00:00:57.422
collaborative.

00:00:57.422 --> 00:00:59.630
And so that's what I really
want to talk about today.

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:02.150
And you know, it really begins
with this changing enterprise.

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:03.774
And I don't think
that's any revelation

00:01:03.774 --> 00:01:06.770
that enterprises are always
constantly under an evolution.

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:08.600
But I think the
current evolution

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:11.270
that we're in really
started back in the '90s,

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:13.460
and started with the
desktop computer.

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:16.760
When the price point hit
such a level and the memory

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:18.430
and processing
power hit a level,

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:20.975
suddenly every desktop was
enabled with a computer.

00:01:20.975 --> 00:01:22.730
It really skyrocketed
the productivity

00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:24.920
of individual user,
empowered our knowledge

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:28.910
workers to really work on their
own to an incredible capacity.

00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:31.400
Jump forward about 10 years,
with the rise of the internet,

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:33.950
and we saw a new trend
starting to take over and drive

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:35.892
that evolution within
the enterprise.

00:01:35.892 --> 00:01:37.600
And that was really
two pieces, actually.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:39.950
It's the rise of the cloud
computing within the internet,

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:42.030
applications that were just
served up over the internet,

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:44.180
taking advantage of that
distribution mechanism,

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:47.450
and the rise of mobile devices,
a supercomputer in your pocket.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:50.030
So suddenly everybody had
a computer in their pocket.

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:52.040
They were networked
together to applications.

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:55.610
And that effect really has
accelerated the productivity

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:57.800
today of, anyway, the
organizations that

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:01.250
have embraced these
types of transformations.

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:05.036
And we've seen the impact
that this second digital

00:02:05.036 --> 00:02:06.660
transformation has
had on the industry.

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:11.270
If you look at the S&amp;P 500 as
kind of an indicator of churn

00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:14.420
within business, it used to
be that once you made the S&amp;P

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:17.690
500, like in the '50s, you'd
stay on there for upwards

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:18.709
of 60 years.

00:02:18.709 --> 00:02:20.750
Not a lot of change there,
because companies-- it

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:22.760
took so much work to get there.

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:25.040
Fast forward to modern times.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:27.200
In 2011, that was
cut down to 18 years.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:29.270
Even today we've seen,
in a matter of weeks,

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:31.570
companies can be in and
out of that S&amp;P 500.

00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:33.560
And it's because
of their ability

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:35.360
to kind of shed a
lot of the things

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:37.960
you needed to build up
over time in the past.

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:41.390
And so when you at how long it
took to get to 50 million users

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:44.240
for the telephone, that
was a 75 year span,

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:47.070
mainly because you had to
build infrastructure, make

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:49.460
the various telephone
companies play nice together,

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:51.168
and really get to that
point where it was

00:02:51.168 --> 00:02:52.605
accessible to 50 million users.

00:02:52.605 --> 00:02:54.980
You see, there's a demarcation
point there in the middle,

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:55.727
cloud innovation.

00:02:55.727 --> 00:02:57.560
Really, that's where
we've been able to shed

00:02:57.560 --> 00:02:58.820
a lot of that
infrastructure and seen

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:01.070
the rise of these applications
that hit the 50 million

00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:04.040
user mark in almost what
appears like no time at all.

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:06.506
Spotify made it in eight
years, Netflix in seven.

00:03:06.506 --> 00:03:07.880
Twitter took that
down to months.

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:11.930
And more recently, Pokemon Go
hit that target in 19 days.

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:15.440
So a real increase
or acceleration

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:18.374
in the churn within businesses.

00:03:18.374 --> 00:03:20.540
And really what that meant
is that organizations now

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:21.590
need to think differently.

00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:23.715
They need to embrace this
digital disruption that's

00:03:23.715 --> 00:03:24.440
happening.

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:25.400
And so they're
turning to companies

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:26.990
like Google, the
digital natives that

00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:30.980
have built tools that were
purposely meant for the Cloud.

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:33.140
And so we're working with
some of the top companies

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:35.670
in the Forbes Cloud 100 list
and bringing these tool sets

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:36.170
to them.

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:39.080
And you may be familiar with
some of the companies here.

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:41.120
A lot of them maybe even
predated that telephone

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:42.710
technology I was talking about.

00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:44.870
Colgate-Palmolive
is a great example

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:46.430
of a company on
the S&amp;P 500 that's

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:49.039
been able to go through this
digital transformation with us.

00:03:49.039 --> 00:03:51.080
It's impacted their culture,
changed the way they

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:54.410
work remotely, given them
access to recruit and retain

00:03:54.410 --> 00:03:57.020
talent that's more used to
working in this digital world.

00:03:57.020 --> 00:03:58.550
And really, more
importantly, it's

00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:00.049
increased the level
of collaboration

00:04:00.049 --> 00:04:01.622
within their organization.

00:04:01.622 --> 00:04:03.080
And they've done
that, like I said,

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:05.050
by embracing the tools
in the G Suite set.

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:07.660
And there's four main
areas within there,

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:10.270
creative tools that empower
your knowledge workers to create

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:12.880
and share content,
connection tools that

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:16.149
bring your company together,
like videoconferencing or email

00:04:16.149 --> 00:04:19.089
and chat, and then
Access tools that

00:04:19.089 --> 00:04:20.709
turn a Google
search into a search

00:04:20.709 --> 00:04:23.116
of your entire enterprise
corpus and allow

00:04:23.116 --> 00:04:25.240
you to surface materials
that you maybe would never

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:26.120
find in the past.

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:28.120
Lastly, that all needs
be managed and controlled

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:30.328
in a secure way by your IT
team, and we'll given them

00:04:30.328 --> 00:04:32.900
the Cloud tools to do that.

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:35.530
But today I want to focus
in on two key elements, one

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:37.740
in the Create side and
one in the Connect side,

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:40.140
and that's the Jamboard
and Google Hangouts Meet.

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:42.220
So the Jamboard, you can
see my partner in crime

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:44.710
here behind me, that's a
digital whiteboard that's

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:46.930
really bringing the
whiteboard experience

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:49.210
into the digital age.

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:51.460
And in Hangouts Meet, a
videoconferencing tool that

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:54.640
really turns any end point,
a phone or a browser,

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:57.230
into a video conferencing tool.

00:04:57.230 --> 00:05:00.000
And you know, the impact that it
has is incredible when we just

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.040
sort of focus on
the transformation

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:03.240
within your meeting rooms.

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:06.180
And it's actually two pieces,
technological and cultural.

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:08.400
And I think that second
one often gets missed.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:11.340
Companies look at the
technology investment

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:14.425
and forget to see that there's
also a cultural impact.

00:05:14.425 --> 00:05:16.800
And so it's not just about
swapping out the tech in order

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:17.880
to provide this
better experience.

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:20.004
You need to transform the
culture of your business.

00:05:20.004 --> 00:05:21.930
And the two go
very hand-in-hand.

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:25.670
What you end up with is a more
engaged community of users.

00:05:25.670 --> 00:05:27.180
They communicate more.

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:29.610
They become highly more
productive and collaborative.

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:31.230
And that results in
an agile business

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:33.810
that can change based on
market or competitive forces

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:37.500
that they're always kind
of trying to adapt to.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:39.480
And so to make up this
toolkit, like I said,

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:41.427
there's the Jamboard
and the Hangouts

00:05:41.427 --> 00:05:42.510
Meet I want to talk about.

00:05:42.510 --> 00:05:44.130
But there's actually
two elements

00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:47.227
to each of those, the software
side and the hardware side.

00:05:47.227 --> 00:05:49.560
The software side, first we'll
talk about Hangouts Meet.

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:51.420
That takes advantage
of that cloud computing

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:54.930
to deliver a high
quality video conference

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:57.031
experience from really
anywhere in the world.

00:05:57.031 --> 00:05:59.280
Of course, in the boardroom,
we want a little bit more

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:02.460
of a professional enterprise
grade video conference,

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:04.800
and so we've come out with
the Meet hardware kit, which

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:08.220
just maximizes the experience
inside of your business

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:10.240
infrastructure.

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:12.100
Now, we felt that
the whiteboard was

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:14.470
a critical piece of
the ideation processing

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:15.970
business in the
past, but it kind of

00:06:15.970 --> 00:06:18.092
failed to move into the future.

00:06:18.092 --> 00:06:19.050
So we brought it there.

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:21.299
That's what the Jamboard is,
a digital whiteboard that

00:06:21.299 --> 00:06:24.940
connects users together
via the board itself

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:27.160
or via series of
applications that

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:29.610
enable you to create and
ideate from anywhere you are.

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:34.216
Now, the result of
implementing these tools

00:06:34.216 --> 00:06:35.840
is kind of fourfold,
and I want to talk

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:37.010
about each one a
little bit separately.

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:38.750
I've harped on the
culture of collaboration.

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:41.166
We're going to talk about why
that's so critical to really

00:06:41.166 --> 00:06:43.910
focus in on creating a face
to face experience and some

00:06:43.910 --> 00:06:46.040
of the benefits that
you get from that.

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:48.440
I also want to talk about
productivity everywhere,

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:50.395
making your users
productive no matter

00:06:50.395 --> 00:06:52.520
where they are in the world,
or what they're doing,

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:54.330
or what time of day.

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:57.100
Next, we'll take a look at
building consensus, driving

00:06:57.100 --> 00:06:59.070
your organization in
the same direction,

00:06:59.070 --> 00:07:01.650
and driving them towards
creative solutions in the less

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:02.817
debate, more create section.

00:07:02.817 --> 00:07:05.275
And then, lastly, I want to
talk about the enterprise grade

00:07:05.275 --> 00:07:07.950
nature of this, the quality
of the tools themselves,

00:07:07.950 --> 00:07:11.140
and your ability to manage
it as an IT organization.

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:13.674
Let's start with the
culture of collaboration.

00:07:13.674 --> 00:07:14.340
Let's be honest.

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:16.987
People really do not
like going to meetings.

00:07:16.987 --> 00:07:19.320
If you've been in any meeting
that was just a conference

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:20.870
bridge, you look
around the table,

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:21.930
everybody's multitasking.

00:07:21.930 --> 00:07:24.200
We found that up
to 60% of people

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:27.330
are actually multitasking in a
traditional conference bridge.

00:07:27.330 --> 00:07:29.790
And you know, it's just
not a productive time

00:07:29.790 --> 00:07:31.440
in terms of meeting itself.

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:33.390
Workers find they have
too many meetings.

00:07:33.390 --> 00:07:36.307
And half of those they've
identified as wasteful.

00:07:36.307 --> 00:07:38.640
And the fact is, there's just
going to be more meetings.

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:39.880
This isn't going away.

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:42.870
There isn't less meetings
than there was yesterday,

00:07:42.870 --> 00:07:43.650
there's more.

00:07:43.650 --> 00:07:46.440
And so as our time gets
consumed more, more meetings,

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:48.400
we need to make these
more productive.

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:51.420
And so Google approached this as
a whole new meeting experience.

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:55.110
We wanted video conference to
be the always thing you did.

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:57.420
There is no reason to
not turn on that camera

00:07:57.420 --> 00:07:59.560
and engage in an
interpersonal way.

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:01.200
So we created Hangout Meet.

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:04.110
And we rebuilt it
from the ground up.

00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:06.540
We improved the media quality,
so the audio and video

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:09.390
is much higher than what you
saw in the initial Hangouts.

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:11.640
We made it easy to record
and share the content

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:13.470
using Drive integrations.

00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:15.337
We've increased the
size of the attendees

00:08:15.337 --> 00:08:16.920
up to 50 participants,
and we're going

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:19.350
to continue to push that
boundary in the future,

00:08:19.350 --> 00:08:21.720
and provided tools like in
meeting context and chat

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:24.120
integrations so that that
in-room experience is

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:27.847
as good as when you're remote.

00:08:27.847 --> 00:08:29.430
And really what this
has done is allow

00:08:29.430 --> 00:08:30.770
you to focus on the meeting.

00:08:30.770 --> 00:08:32.811
And so it's brought people
back into the meeting.

00:08:32.811 --> 00:08:36.510
Instead of multitasking
and doing other things,

00:08:36.510 --> 00:08:37.740
you have presence.

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:39.720
People have to be a
part of that meeting.

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:43.320
There's a social contract
built in to being face to face.

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:45.660
Not only that, I'm getting
a lot more information

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:49.110
via body language and even be
able to cross language barriers

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:53.640
almost, because I'm able to
see the emotions and the face

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:55.680
reactions of the
people involved.

00:08:55.680 --> 00:08:58.080
But actually, the
most important impact

00:08:58.080 --> 00:08:59.847
is one that we
actually discovered

00:08:59.847 --> 00:09:00.930
through a lot of research.

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:03.030
So Google spent a lot
of time figuring out

00:09:03.030 --> 00:09:04.290
what makes a team successful.

00:09:04.290 --> 00:09:06.570
And actually, you might
see a hyperlink there.

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:08.667
If you Google
re:Work and Google,

00:09:08.667 --> 00:09:11.250
you'll come across a series of
studies that we did just trying

00:09:11.250 --> 00:09:12.416
to improve our organization.

00:09:12.416 --> 00:09:14.522
We've shared that out to
the rest of the world.

00:09:14.522 --> 00:09:16.230
And one study in
particular, like I said,

00:09:16.230 --> 00:09:18.180
was focused on making
successful teams.

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:20.760
And what we found
time and again was

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:24.420
it wasn't having common goals
or having strong leadership

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:26.940
or clear tasks defined
with timelines,

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:29.620
it was something called
psychological safety.

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:31.135
That's what made
a team successful.

00:09:31.135 --> 00:09:32.760
And what we mean by
that is the ability

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:35.190
to feel safe in
sharing your ideas,

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:37.890
taking risks, and really
working as a team.

00:09:37.890 --> 00:09:40.211
That's something that's
built into a video conference

00:09:40.211 --> 00:09:40.710
culture.

00:09:40.710 --> 00:09:43.620
And it can't be a culture
that meets on video conference

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:46.230
once a week for
that weekly meeting.

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:49.420
It's a company that meets all
the time on video conference,

00:09:49.420 --> 00:09:51.540
that uses video conference
as the go to way

00:09:51.540 --> 00:09:53.580
to communicate when you're
not in the same room

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:55.920
and creates an experience
like you're in the same room

00:09:55.920 --> 00:09:57.120
no matter where you are.

00:09:57.120 --> 00:09:59.440
And so I think that's
something to really highlight,

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:02.940
there's subtle psychological
benefits and social benefits

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:04.500
that come out of
this that really

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:07.170
make this an impactful
cultural transformation.

00:10:07.170 --> 00:10:10.440
And with all of those, you begin
to grasp the idea of business

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:11.070
in real time.

00:10:11.070 --> 00:10:12.510
And this is something,
again, that kind of

00:10:12.510 --> 00:10:14.820
came with the rise of cloud
computing, these interconnected

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:15.330
systems.

00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:17.640
that were able to update
databases in real time.

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:19.980
Well, that's now moved
on to the getting things

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:21.600
done part of the business.

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:24.060
And it's being able to
jump on a video conference

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:27.600
and tackle a problem without
having to pre-establish when

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:29.070
that conference
is going to be in

00:10:29.070 --> 00:10:30.660
and set that up in
a tool somewhere,

00:10:30.660 --> 00:10:33.201
or be in that meeting room where
we have the video conference

00:10:33.201 --> 00:10:34.020
unit set up.

00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:36.126
It's about just jumping
in and getting it done.

00:10:36.126 --> 00:10:37.750
It's about doing real
time on Jamboard,

00:10:37.750 --> 00:10:40.600
so I can share my ideas and
collaborate in real time.

00:10:40.600 --> 00:10:43.982
I can fix, maybe, something
that somebody made a mistake on.

00:10:43.982 --> 00:10:46.440
You know, before, I was trapped
behind the video conference

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:46.940
tool.

00:10:46.940 --> 00:10:49.260
Now I can just update
that on my Jamboard,

00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:51.000
they see the impact on theirs.

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:53.190
So it's about just
increasing the acceleration

00:10:53.190 --> 00:10:56.080
of how you get things done.

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:57.840
Now, of course, once
you're doing this,

00:10:57.840 --> 00:10:59.260
you need to be able
to do this everywhere.

00:10:59.260 --> 00:11:01.740
The way you drive these tools
to be part of your culture

00:11:01.740 --> 00:11:04.090
is by making everybody
enabled to use them no matter

00:11:04.090 --> 00:11:06.090
where they are, if they're
at home, on the road,

00:11:06.090 --> 00:11:07.120
or anywhere else.

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:09.480
And the fact is, employees
are more and more remote

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:10.320
as we go on.

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:12.450
You know, we've sort
of done the math,

00:11:12.450 --> 00:11:14.520
and by 2020 half
of your workforce

00:11:14.520 --> 00:11:17.070
is going to be contributing
remotely, whether that's

00:11:17.070 --> 00:11:20.695
on client sites or touching
in from their vacations--

00:11:20.695 --> 00:11:23.070
not something you want to do,
but something more and more

00:11:23.070 --> 00:11:25.060
we have to do in this
ever connected world.

00:11:25.060 --> 00:11:26.574
So we need the tools
to support that

00:11:26.574 --> 00:11:28.740
so that things don't go off
the rails when we're not

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:29.844
physically in the office.

00:11:29.844 --> 00:11:31.260
And that's really
what we've tried

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:34.170
to do with these
conference tools,

00:11:34.170 --> 00:11:37.090
make them super easy to join
from a browser or a tablet

00:11:37.090 --> 00:11:40.430
or a smartphone, make it as
simple as tapping a URL to hop

00:11:40.430 --> 00:11:43.740
in the meeting, providing a
dial-in code at no extra charge

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:46.860
so that you can dial into
the meeting through a phone.

00:11:46.860 --> 00:11:48.540
That's part of our
Enterprise Edition.

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:50.880
And then tying that all
into the Calendar tool

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:53.370
so that when I book a
room, I go in there,

00:11:53.370 --> 00:11:54.840
the meeting's
already in that room.

00:11:54.840 --> 00:11:57.420
Anybody added to the Calendar
gets access to the meeting,

00:11:57.420 --> 00:12:00.060
gets access to the content
that's part of that meeting.

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:03.360
Really just trying to make
it as easy as possible and as

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:05.690
beneficial for all your users.

00:12:05.690 --> 00:12:07.440
We worked with the
Wyoming Medical Center,

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:09.390
and they leveraged
this technology

00:12:09.390 --> 00:12:12.060
to reach out to their
clinicians in remote areas,

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:14.610
connect those to neurosurgeons
and cardiologists,

00:12:14.610 --> 00:12:16.596
and allow them to
spread knowledge way

00:12:16.596 --> 00:12:18.220
beyond the walls of
their organization.

00:12:18.220 --> 00:12:20.880
No longer did they have to bring
them in for knowledge transfer

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:21.630
sessions.

00:12:21.630 --> 00:12:23.020
In the moment, when
they needed the answer,

00:12:23.020 --> 00:12:25.436
they could jump on a Hangout
right from their mobile phone

00:12:25.436 --> 00:12:28.335
and be connected
to these experts.

00:12:28.335 --> 00:12:30.960
And that's really what it takes,
having a suite of tools that's

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:33.420
good on the go but also
in the boardroom, bridges

00:12:33.420 --> 00:12:35.230
that gap between
remote and on location.

00:12:39.470 --> 00:12:40.910
So with all these
tools in place,

00:12:40.910 --> 00:12:43.111
we've got everybody
collaborating and working

00:12:43.111 --> 00:12:43.610
together.

00:12:43.610 --> 00:12:45.443
They're productive no
matter where they are.

00:12:45.443 --> 00:12:48.530
The result is this
idea of consensus.

00:12:48.530 --> 00:12:51.660
But what we found was, when
we got the video conferencing

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:54.800
so successful at Google, we'd
left behind the whiteboard.

00:12:54.800 --> 00:12:56.759
And the whiteboard had
become this broken tool.

00:12:56.759 --> 00:12:58.549
It worked great for
the people in the room.

00:12:58.549 --> 00:12:59.930
It still worked
like it just did.

00:12:59.930 --> 00:13:01.400
Pick up the marker and go.

00:13:01.400 --> 00:13:04.024
But for somebody remote
on the video conference,

00:13:04.024 --> 00:13:05.690
they'd have to play
the broken telephone

00:13:05.690 --> 00:13:07.400
game to get that
picture corrected,

00:13:07.400 --> 00:13:10.130
or maybe you just never got
to add their ideas whatsoever.

00:13:10.130 --> 00:13:11.990
And, you know, we tried
hacking a solution.

00:13:11.990 --> 00:13:13.989
I'm sure everybody's done
a few of these things,

00:13:13.989 --> 00:13:16.460
maybe taken a picture of the
whiteboard and e-mailed that

00:13:16.460 --> 00:13:18.792
around to everybody, or
pointed a camera from a video

00:13:18.792 --> 00:13:20.250
conference unit at
the whiteboard--

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:22.910
hopefully those remote people
could see my scratchy writing

00:13:22.910 --> 00:13:23.750
on the board--

00:13:23.750 --> 00:13:26.540
or tried using Docs
and Sheets, which

00:13:26.540 --> 00:13:28.670
are an outstanding
form a collaboration,

00:13:28.670 --> 00:13:30.890
but are structured and
kind of put you in a box,

00:13:30.890 --> 00:13:33.710
and don't allow that freeform
creativity of the Jamboard

00:13:33.710 --> 00:13:35.000
or of a whiteboard.

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:37.250
And so that's why we
built the Jamboard.

00:13:37.250 --> 00:13:40.160
We spent a lot of time
building both the hardware

00:13:40.160 --> 00:13:42.830
and the software to make this
a wonderful whiteboard-esque

00:13:42.830 --> 00:13:43.890
experience.

00:13:43.890 --> 00:13:46.580
It allows you to contribute
anywhere the way you want,

00:13:46.580 --> 00:13:49.760
and it really engages your
team to work better, no matter

00:13:49.760 --> 00:13:52.150
where they're located.

00:13:52.150 --> 00:13:54.650
And the first thing you're going
to notice, the whiteboard's

00:13:54.650 --> 00:13:56.305
kind of a journey in my mind.

00:13:56.305 --> 00:13:58.430
You kind of walk up, and
the first thing you notice

00:13:58.430 --> 00:14:00.814
is how smooth it reacts
to the marker, how much it

00:14:00.814 --> 00:14:02.480
feels like I actually
picked up a marker

00:14:02.480 --> 00:14:03.620
and wrote on the board.

00:14:03.620 --> 00:14:06.920
But then you start to notice
that this Jamboard does

00:14:06.920 --> 00:14:09.020
a lot more than my
whiteboard used to.

00:14:09.020 --> 00:14:11.630
It corrects my writing
when I write messy.

00:14:11.630 --> 00:14:15.260
It doesn't need to be taken a
picture of or written "do not

00:14:15.260 --> 00:14:18.530
erase," because it autosaves
every time I do something.

00:14:18.530 --> 00:14:20.810
It's tied to rich content,
so I can bring images out

00:14:20.810 --> 00:14:23.000
of a browser or from my Drive.

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:26.300
And it has machine learning
to correct my crappy drawings

00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:30.060
and turn them into something
a lot more appealing.

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:31.890
It's connected together,
so one Jamboard

00:14:31.890 --> 00:14:33.450
connects to another Jamboard.

00:14:33.450 --> 00:14:35.427
If I'm working with
somebody on the other side

00:14:35.427 --> 00:14:38.010
of the continent, I can see what
they're drawing in real time.

00:14:38.010 --> 00:14:40.260
They can see what I
draw in real time.

00:14:40.260 --> 00:14:41.880
It works on mobile
devices, so it

00:14:41.880 --> 00:14:44.140
doesn't matter how
I'm connecting in,

00:14:44.140 --> 00:14:45.570
I'm still sharing
that experience

00:14:45.570 --> 00:14:49.320
and ideating, never
missing a creative moment.

00:14:49.320 --> 00:14:51.090
It works great with
Hangouts, so if I

00:14:51.090 --> 00:14:52.860
have people that
aren't near a Jamboard,

00:14:52.860 --> 00:14:55.470
maybe don't use any Google
applications whatsoever,

00:14:55.470 --> 00:14:58.590
they can click that Hangouts
Meet link, jump into Hangout,

00:14:58.590 --> 00:15:01.920
see the Jamboard session, and
provide their verbal feedback.

00:15:01.920 --> 00:15:05.712
So a great pairing of the
Jamboard and Hangouts Meet.

00:15:05.712 --> 00:15:07.170
If they're paired
in a room they're

00:15:07.170 --> 00:15:11.560
going to work in sync to
join the meeting together.

00:15:11.560 --> 00:15:15.280
And maybe most importantly,
that connection to G Suite.

00:15:15.280 --> 00:15:17.820
So G Suite offers a lot of
really great collaborative

00:15:17.820 --> 00:15:18.550
editors.

00:15:18.550 --> 00:15:20.570
We've added Jamboard
to that mix.

00:15:20.570 --> 00:15:23.140
But we've also added
those editors to Jamboard.

00:15:23.140 --> 00:15:25.450
So I can bring in a
Doc, Sheet, or Slide

00:15:25.450 --> 00:15:28.412
to my Jamboard session, start
to annotate, add my notes,

00:15:28.412 --> 00:15:30.370
really collaborate around
that content as well,

00:15:30.370 --> 00:15:32.024
kind of bridging
that workflow gap.

00:15:34.839 --> 00:15:36.880
Lastly, I want to talk
about the enterprise grade

00:15:36.880 --> 00:15:37.504
nature of this.

00:15:37.504 --> 00:15:39.910
And we spent a lot of
time really focusing in

00:15:39.910 --> 00:15:43.480
on how to make these tools the
enterprise experience not only

00:15:43.480 --> 00:15:46.310
the users needed, but that IT
needed to manage and support

00:15:46.310 --> 00:15:46.810
them.

00:15:46.810 --> 00:15:50.890
And so we added a lot of
enterprise grade functionality.

00:15:50.890 --> 00:15:53.952
We took advantage of Google's
robust security posture

00:15:53.952 --> 00:15:55.910
and really took advantage
of our scale as well.

00:15:55.910 --> 00:15:57.920
And let's start with
the scale question.

00:15:57.920 --> 00:16:00.640
You know, we ride on the
same network and data center

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:02.100
infrastructure as YouTube.

00:16:02.100 --> 00:16:04.870
So we're able to take
advantage of that global scale

00:16:04.870 --> 00:16:09.049
to deliver high quality video
conferencing around the world.

00:16:09.049 --> 00:16:11.590
We've integrated this in with
G Suite in a few different ways

00:16:11.590 --> 00:16:14.090
beyond just the integration
I just talked about.

00:16:14.090 --> 00:16:15.917
But it begins with
your Calendar.

00:16:15.917 --> 00:16:17.500
So when I start
Calendaring something,

00:16:17.500 --> 00:16:19.510
it will suggest
rooms for me based

00:16:19.510 --> 00:16:20.800
on the location of the users.

00:16:20.800 --> 00:16:22.390
Then it will
automatically add a link

00:16:22.390 --> 00:16:24.610
into a Hangouts Meet session.

00:16:24.610 --> 00:16:27.130
It'll automatically add that
to the room I put it in,

00:16:27.130 --> 00:16:28.480
so I just have to tap and go.

00:16:28.480 --> 00:16:30.070
Really just makes
that a seamless,

00:16:30.070 --> 00:16:31.884
frictionless experience
for my users.

00:16:31.884 --> 00:16:33.550
When they walk in the
room, the Jamboard

00:16:33.550 --> 00:16:36.290
will ask to join the meeting
if it's paired with the device.

00:16:36.290 --> 00:16:39.210
If I decide to record that
session, I tap a button

00:16:39.210 --> 00:16:41.510
and that's dropped into
my Drive for me later on.

00:16:41.510 --> 00:16:44.950
And all of that, the devices,
the experience for the users,

00:16:44.950 --> 00:16:46.810
and the licensing,
is all managed inside

00:16:46.810 --> 00:16:48.610
of the Admin console
in the Cloud,

00:16:48.610 --> 00:16:52.439
so I can easily access and
make changes in real time.

00:16:52.439 --> 00:16:54.480
Let's take a look at some
of the hardware itself.

00:16:54.480 --> 00:16:57.090
So this is our Hangouts
Meet hardware kit.

00:16:57.090 --> 00:16:59.820
It comes with four pieces;
the Chromebox, which you see

00:16:59.820 --> 00:17:02.562
on the left there, a camera--

00:17:02.562 --> 00:17:04.020
depending on the
size of your room,

00:17:04.020 --> 00:17:07.170
we'll talk about that in
just a second-- a Speakermic,

00:17:07.170 --> 00:17:11.079
and a touchscreen to control
everything about the meeting.

00:17:11.079 --> 00:17:12.736
So this would be
your in-room setup.

00:17:12.736 --> 00:17:14.819
And let's just talk a bit
about each one of those.

00:17:14.819 --> 00:17:17.582
This small Huddly camera
is really powerful.

00:17:17.582 --> 00:17:19.290
It packs a big punch
for a little camera,

00:17:19.290 --> 00:17:20.430
has a wide field of view.

00:17:20.430 --> 00:17:22.859
It's ideal for the smaller
rooms that you have.

00:17:22.859 --> 00:17:24.599
It'll capture
everybody in the room.

00:17:24.599 --> 00:17:26.980
Not only that, it has
onboard machine learning.

00:17:26.980 --> 00:17:29.850
So we've put machine learning
into the camera, which

00:17:29.850 --> 00:17:32.892
currently enables
us to crop and zoom

00:17:32.892 --> 00:17:35.100
to get the right perspective
for the number of people

00:17:35.100 --> 00:17:35.790
in the room.

00:17:35.790 --> 00:17:37.620
And we'll power
even more machine

00:17:37.620 --> 00:17:41.440
learning as we move forward
and develop this out further.

00:17:41.440 --> 00:17:44.830
We've also got a large
room kit as well.

00:17:44.830 --> 00:17:49.030
That comes with a high
quality PTZ Logitech camera,

00:17:49.030 --> 00:17:53.530
offers 10x zoom in high HD,
and also a wide field of view

00:17:53.530 --> 00:17:54.700
there.

00:17:54.700 --> 00:17:57.460
The Speakermic took a lot
of engineering and effort

00:17:57.460 --> 00:17:59.410
to create a state of the
art noise suppression

00:17:59.410 --> 00:18:01.420
and echo cancellation
experience in the room,

00:18:01.420 --> 00:18:03.520
but also allows you to
fully control the room

00:18:03.520 --> 00:18:04.930
from any one of the devices.

00:18:04.930 --> 00:18:07.600
You can daisy chain these
together with a single cable

00:18:07.600 --> 00:18:10.406
to extend it out for
large configurations.

00:18:10.406 --> 00:18:12.280
All the updates to this
are passed down right

00:18:12.280 --> 00:18:14.260
from the Chromebox, so I don't
even have to manage this.

00:18:14.260 --> 00:18:16.510
As we come up with better
algorithms for that noise

00:18:16.510 --> 00:18:18.468
suppression, they're
going to automatically get

00:18:18.468 --> 00:18:20.682
updated into that device.

00:18:20.682 --> 00:18:22.890
And the center of it all,
though, is the touchscreen.

00:18:22.890 --> 00:18:25.496
And this is a really great
experience for your end users.

00:18:25.496 --> 00:18:27.120
They don't have to
worry about how they

00:18:27.120 --> 00:18:28.360
connect into their meeting.

00:18:28.360 --> 00:18:31.120
They just walk in, tap their
meeting on the Calendar there.

00:18:31.120 --> 00:18:32.286
And that's what's happening.

00:18:32.286 --> 00:18:35.190
It's being fed from the Calendar
booking right into the room.

00:18:35.190 --> 00:18:37.350
I can see my
meeting, tap and go.

00:18:37.350 --> 00:18:39.560
Want to record the meeting,
I tap it once more.

00:18:39.560 --> 00:18:42.527
Want to call a phone, I can dial
a phone number out from there,

00:18:42.527 --> 00:18:44.610
bring up the chat that's
going on for the meeting,

00:18:44.610 --> 00:18:46.610
all sorts of functionality
built right in there,

00:18:46.610 --> 00:18:49.410
easy for your
users to engage in.

00:18:49.410 --> 00:18:51.790
We've started a qualified
peripherals program

00:18:51.790 --> 00:18:54.610
recently to just increase
the amount of customization

00:18:54.610 --> 00:18:56.770
you can do to your rooms,
adding different cable

00:18:56.770 --> 00:18:59.200
sets, some different
camera options,

00:18:59.200 --> 00:19:01.840
and really just trying to
give you more options in terms

00:19:01.840 --> 00:19:04.555
of how you build out those
more complex or unusual rooms

00:19:04.555 --> 00:19:06.770
that you're facing.

00:19:06.770 --> 00:19:09.050
On the Jamboard side,
we spent a lot of time

00:19:09.050 --> 00:19:12.110
building out the
quality of the screen,

00:19:12.110 --> 00:19:14.180
so that the touch was
really responsive,

00:19:14.180 --> 00:19:16.985
but also you get this big
screen for a low price point.

00:19:16.985 --> 00:19:18.860
So that took a lot of
the engineering effort,

00:19:18.860 --> 00:19:21.680
was to make this an
easily engagable,

00:19:21.680 --> 00:19:23.360
feel like a fluid whiteboard.

00:19:23.360 --> 00:19:25.569
But we also took some time
and effort into the stylus

00:19:25.569 --> 00:19:27.859
to make sure you didn't have
to have batteries in here,

00:19:27.859 --> 00:19:30.710
so that nothing would get in the
way of you hosting your meeting

00:19:30.710 --> 00:19:33.901
or running your meeting.

00:19:33.901 --> 00:19:35.400
All of this is
managed, like I said,

00:19:35.400 --> 00:19:37.040
through that centralized
administration console.

00:19:37.040 --> 00:19:39.415
So we're bringing that all
into the Google Admin console,

00:19:39.415 --> 00:19:42.897
where you manage your
G Suite users today.

00:19:42.897 --> 00:19:44.480
And we're providing
you with analytics

00:19:44.480 --> 00:19:46.605
that tell you things about
your resource allocation

00:19:46.605 --> 00:19:49.850
and help you manage
cost control as well.

00:19:49.850 --> 00:19:51.350
So now what I want
to do is actually

00:19:51.350 --> 00:19:53.240
show you a little bit
about the Jamboard

00:19:53.240 --> 00:19:55.070
and see what that
experience is like.

00:19:55.070 --> 00:19:57.950
But just a quick
reminder that these tools

00:19:57.950 --> 00:20:00.350
really do a few different
things for your organization.

00:20:00.350 --> 00:20:03.920
First is to change the culture
of how you guys work together.

00:20:03.920 --> 00:20:06.020
One of the phenomena
I have at Google

00:20:06.020 --> 00:20:07.970
is when I've worked
with somebody

00:20:07.970 --> 00:20:09.890
through a few video
conferences, and then I

00:20:09.890 --> 00:20:11.570
end up meeting them in person.

00:20:11.570 --> 00:20:13.536
And 9 times out of
10, I forget that it's

00:20:13.536 --> 00:20:15.410
the first time I've
actually met this person,

00:20:15.410 --> 00:20:18.079
because the video conference
experience feels so

00:20:18.079 --> 00:20:19.370
much like getting to know them.

00:20:19.370 --> 00:20:21.380
It just creates a tighter
work relationship.

00:20:21.380 --> 00:20:23.130
Changing that culture
of your organization

00:20:23.130 --> 00:20:26.120
is one of the top
benefits that you

00:20:26.120 --> 00:20:28.790
get from using the Hangouts
Meet and the Jamboard tools.

00:20:28.790 --> 00:20:31.070
But it also makes you
productive everywhere.

00:20:31.070 --> 00:20:33.409
If you need to be
remote for a day,

00:20:33.409 --> 00:20:35.450
you can easily still
interact with your coworkers

00:20:35.450 --> 00:20:37.610
or your customers
or your partners,

00:20:37.610 --> 00:20:40.790
using Hangouts Meet as if
you were in the same room.

00:20:40.790 --> 00:20:43.880
Those together drive
consensus, put your company

00:20:43.880 --> 00:20:45.010
on the same page.

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:47.218
And it can all be managed
through an enterprise grade

00:20:47.218 --> 00:20:51.090
toolset to make sure you're safe
and secure and in compliance.

00:20:51.090 --> 00:20:53.130
So with that, let's
move to the Jamboard.

00:20:53.130 --> 00:20:56.040
Now, I'm kind of lucky today,
because I have an Android

00:20:56.040 --> 00:20:59.700
enabled laptop, and I have
a Jamboard here behind me.

00:20:59.700 --> 00:21:02.220
So you guys are going
to see a couple of ways

00:21:02.220 --> 00:21:04.780
that I can interact with
both devices at once.

00:21:04.780 --> 00:21:07.295
I'm going to jump into
my Jamboard on the screen

00:21:07.295 --> 00:21:08.670
here, so that you
can kind of see

00:21:08.670 --> 00:21:10.770
what it's like when
I'm editing and see

00:21:10.770 --> 00:21:13.620
some of the editing tools
that are built into it

00:21:13.620 --> 00:21:14.830
and all that sort of stuff.

00:21:14.830 --> 00:21:16.320
So I'm going to do a
little bit of drawing.

00:21:16.320 --> 00:21:18.120
On the left here,
you probably can see

00:21:18.120 --> 00:21:20.417
I've got a whole set of tools.

00:21:20.417 --> 00:21:22.500
And I can kind of flip
those around really easily.

00:21:22.500 --> 00:21:25.416
It gives me a lot of power to do
different sort of interactions

00:21:25.416 --> 00:21:26.040
with the board.

00:21:26.040 --> 00:21:27.414
I'm going to be
using that a lot.

00:21:27.414 --> 00:21:30.240
I've also got my friendly Undo,
so if I ever mess anything up,

00:21:30.240 --> 00:21:32.100
I can just tap that a
couple of times and it

00:21:32.100 --> 00:21:33.857
takes me back to where I was.

00:21:33.857 --> 00:21:36.190
Now I want to talk to you
about the collaborative nature

00:21:36.190 --> 00:21:37.565
of Jamboard,
because to me that's

00:21:37.565 --> 00:21:39.010
the true power of this tool.

00:21:39.010 --> 00:21:42.390
The fact that it does the
cool whiteboarding stuff is

00:21:42.390 --> 00:21:44.760
great and awesome, but really
where it gets its legs,

00:21:44.760 --> 00:21:47.135
in my mind, is when it starts
to be a collaborative tool.

00:21:47.135 --> 00:21:56.740
But we designed it to
be easy and accessible.

00:21:56.740 --> 00:21:58.450
And what I mean by
that is, you shouldn't

00:21:58.450 --> 00:22:01.097
have to go through
training to use a Jamboard.

00:22:01.097 --> 00:22:02.680
First time I saw a
Jamboard, actually,

00:22:02.680 --> 00:22:04.480
was Bring your Kids
to Work Day, and it

00:22:04.480 --> 00:22:07.612
was amazing to watch these
8 and 9-year-olds run up

00:22:07.612 --> 00:22:09.520
to the Jamboard, grab
one of the stylus,

00:22:09.520 --> 00:22:11.560
and start marking up
the board right away,

00:22:11.560 --> 00:22:13.870
grabbing images off the
web, putting funny mustaches

00:22:13.870 --> 00:22:14.540
on people.

00:22:14.540 --> 00:22:17.470
And it just showed me how easy
and approachable this device

00:22:17.470 --> 00:22:20.290
was, and also from anywhere.

00:22:20.290 --> 00:22:25.689
Because then, when you start to
connect the Jamboard together,

00:22:25.689 --> 00:22:27.480
you start to see that
collaborative nature.

00:22:27.480 --> 00:22:29.520
Now, you may have noticed
that the Jamboard was

00:22:29.520 --> 00:22:31.002
fixing my writing as we went.

00:22:31.002 --> 00:22:32.460
That's part of the
machine learning

00:22:32.460 --> 00:22:33.584
that's built into the tool.

00:22:33.584 --> 00:22:35.400
So I was using
handwriting recognition

00:22:35.400 --> 00:22:37.650
to take my messy kind
of doctor's writing

00:22:37.650 --> 00:22:40.230
and turn that into something
legible for everybody to read.

00:22:40.230 --> 00:22:42.115
Really cool aspect
of the Jamboard.

00:22:42.115 --> 00:22:43.490
But I also have
a few other tools

00:22:43.490 --> 00:22:45.115
I want show off, just
while we're here.

00:22:45.115 --> 00:22:46.232
I can lasso things.

00:22:46.232 --> 00:22:48.690
Each one of these is an object
that I can then move around.

00:22:48.690 --> 00:22:50.400
In this case, I'm just going to
kind of shrink that and put it

00:22:50.400 --> 00:22:51.790
up in the corner.

00:22:51.790 --> 00:22:55.140
So I have that ability
to reconfigure the board,

00:22:55.140 --> 00:22:56.517
very unlike a whiteboard.

00:22:56.517 --> 00:22:58.350
I don't have to erase
and redraw everything.

00:22:58.350 --> 00:22:59.600
I can just move things around.

00:22:59.600 --> 00:23:01.349
Same with this picture
here in the middle.

00:23:01.349 --> 00:23:03.400
I'm just going to move
it down a little bit.

00:23:03.400 --> 00:23:05.880
Now, like I said, I want
to talk about the way

00:23:05.880 --> 00:23:07.760
to use Jamboard as a
collaborative tool,

00:23:07.760 --> 00:23:10.960
as an extension of the
collaboration you're doing in G

00:23:10.960 --> 00:23:12.510
Suite and other tools today.

00:23:12.510 --> 00:23:15.990
And really, when you see that
sort of aspect of Jamboard

00:23:15.990 --> 00:23:19.770
best is when you bring a
second Jamboard into the mix.

00:23:19.770 --> 00:23:21.947
So I've added a second
picture of my Jamboard

00:23:21.947 --> 00:23:22.780
there on the screen.

00:23:22.780 --> 00:23:25.590
And if you notice behind
me, you can see in real time

00:23:25.590 --> 00:23:28.900
the Jamboard's updating
what's going on.

00:23:28.900 --> 00:23:31.650
And so when I have two Jamboards
together, I can connect them,

00:23:31.650 --> 00:23:33.360
and I begin to mirror
what's happening

00:23:33.360 --> 00:23:36.010
on one Jamboard with the other.

00:23:36.010 --> 00:23:38.430
So that means that
if I'm on one coast,

00:23:38.430 --> 00:23:41.880
and I'm working with somebody
maybe out in California--

00:23:41.880 --> 00:23:43.760
I'm in New York today--

00:23:43.760 --> 00:23:44.660
I can share ideas.

00:23:44.660 --> 00:23:48.020
So maybe I'm drawing the
next great idea for Google

00:23:48.020 --> 00:23:50.110
on the board.

00:23:50.110 --> 00:23:54.780
They can jump in, add their
ideas, extend my idea,

00:23:54.780 --> 00:23:58.980
correct my idea, and really
turn this into a reality.

00:23:58.980 --> 00:24:02.049
Now, you may see on the bottom
of the screen right now,

00:24:02.049 --> 00:24:04.590
the machine learning is trying
to process and figure out what

00:24:04.590 --> 00:24:06.040
it is I was actually drawing.

00:24:06.040 --> 00:24:07.500
It chose a light bulb.

00:24:07.500 --> 00:24:10.100
I can just tap that
picture on the bottom,

00:24:10.100 --> 00:24:12.240
and it's now fixed
up my messy drawing

00:24:12.240 --> 00:24:13.627
and, again, made
it more legible.

00:24:13.627 --> 00:24:15.210
I'm a little surprised
it even thought

00:24:15.210 --> 00:24:17.790
that was a light bulb,
because it was very terrible.

00:24:17.790 --> 00:24:19.831
But you can see the power
of the machine learning

00:24:19.831 --> 00:24:23.070
to just take an unrecognizable
kind of chicken scratch

00:24:23.070 --> 00:24:25.930
and turn it into
something impactful.

00:24:25.930 --> 00:24:27.720
So like I said, the
power of the Jamboards

00:24:27.720 --> 00:24:28.860
is when you connect
them together.

00:24:28.860 --> 00:24:30.630
But what if you're not in
a room with a Jamboard?

00:24:30.630 --> 00:24:32.420
What if you're on
the road somewhere?

00:24:32.420 --> 00:24:37.050
Well, we've provided a complete
toolkit of applications.

00:24:37.050 --> 00:24:41.940
So I might want to use that
on my mobile device, my phone,

00:24:41.940 --> 00:24:43.030
or a tablet.

00:24:43.030 --> 00:24:46.670
Or maybe I'm going to be
connecting via my laptop.

00:24:46.670 --> 00:24:50.880
Let's take a look at each one
of those use cases a little bit

00:24:50.880 --> 00:24:52.140
separately.

00:24:52.140 --> 00:24:55.920
So with the phone, it's maybe
not the ideal space to draw in.

00:24:55.920 --> 00:24:58.310
It's a little tricky,
limited real estate.

00:24:58.310 --> 00:25:00.510
But it is a great
place to ideate still.

00:25:00.510 --> 00:25:03.840
I can still see everything
that's going on on the Jamboard

00:25:03.840 --> 00:25:05.190
right from my phone.

00:25:05.190 --> 00:25:08.490
And I do have the ability
to also add content

00:25:08.490 --> 00:25:09.520
to the Jam session.

00:25:09.520 --> 00:25:11.070
And one of the ways
that I like best

00:25:11.070 --> 00:25:12.490
is by leveraging sticky notes.

00:25:12.490 --> 00:25:16.010
And a very popular way
of solving problems today

00:25:16.010 --> 00:25:18.990
is called Human Oriented Design,
and it leverages sticky notes

00:25:18.990 --> 00:25:20.040
very intensely.

00:25:20.040 --> 00:25:22.320
You kind of place them on
quadrants and prioritize

00:25:22.320 --> 00:25:22.960
problems.

00:25:22.960 --> 00:25:25.770
And so if I have a
great idea, I can just

00:25:25.770 --> 00:25:28.020
type that on my
sticky note and add

00:25:28.020 --> 00:25:29.280
that right in the Jam session.

00:25:29.280 --> 00:25:31.321
And now I have another
asset on the screen, which

00:25:31.321 --> 00:25:33.900
is my sticky note, and let's
just put that down there.

00:25:33.900 --> 00:25:36.150
And I'll show it, just going
to go right from my phone

00:25:36.150 --> 00:25:37.800
into that Jam session.

00:25:37.800 --> 00:25:39.884
And if you're familiar
with Human Oriented Design,

00:25:39.884 --> 00:25:41.633
sometimes it's a little
bit of a challenge

00:25:41.633 --> 00:25:43.500
to get everybody
together for that session

00:25:43.500 --> 00:25:45.480
or to get everybody
in front of the board,

00:25:45.480 --> 00:25:47.370
because maybe some
people are a little shy

00:25:47.370 --> 00:25:49.240
or there's just
too much going on.

00:25:49.240 --> 00:25:52.050
This allows me to use my
phone as that mechanism

00:25:52.050 --> 00:25:53.670
for participating
in the Jam session.

00:25:53.670 --> 00:25:56.390
Really powerful way
of extending that.

00:25:56.390 --> 00:25:59.870
Now, on my tablet, I have
even more functionality.

00:25:59.870 --> 00:26:02.740
So the tablet actually
does everything

00:26:02.740 --> 00:26:06.270
that my Jamboard does
in the palm of my hands.

00:26:06.270 --> 00:26:10.770
And so I'm able to put
content onto the Jamboard,

00:26:10.770 --> 00:26:13.050
but also see what's going on.

00:26:13.050 --> 00:26:15.150
I basically have a
miniature Jamboard.

00:26:15.150 --> 00:26:17.160
And not only that, but
I actually don't even

00:26:17.160 --> 00:26:19.260
need the Jamboard at all.

00:26:19.260 --> 00:26:21.570
I can have a Jam
session directly

00:26:21.570 --> 00:26:24.180
with somebody on a mobile
device, maybe another tablet

00:26:24.180 --> 00:26:25.290
or a phone.

00:26:25.290 --> 00:26:30.060
And I can even push stuff
out to somebody on a laptop.

00:26:30.060 --> 00:26:33.630
On the laptop, I can use
the Jam viewer to see what's

00:26:33.630 --> 00:26:35.730
going on in the Jam session.

00:26:35.730 --> 00:26:39.420
I can also join maybe
a video conference

00:26:39.420 --> 00:26:44.067
and share my screen to
share that Jam that way.

00:26:44.067 --> 00:26:46.400
Now, since we're talking about
sharing our Jam sessions,

00:26:46.400 --> 00:26:47.730
let's add a little
bit more content.

00:26:47.730 --> 00:26:49.940
And this is where the rich
media starts to come into play.

00:26:49.940 --> 00:26:51.940
I've got a couple of tools
here on the left hand

00:26:51.940 --> 00:26:55.100
side that allow me to
grab images from the web.

00:26:55.100 --> 00:26:58.130
I'm going to grab a picture
of the Hangouts logo.

00:26:58.130 --> 00:27:00.860
And I can just use
that image browser

00:27:00.860 --> 00:27:02.750
to drag images right
onto the screen.

00:27:02.750 --> 00:27:06.110
So there I've just
dragged that Hangouts logo

00:27:06.110 --> 00:27:07.640
onto my Jam session.

00:27:07.640 --> 00:27:10.119
But I also have a
regular web browser.

00:27:10.119 --> 00:27:11.660
Let's look up Hangouts
Meet hardware.

00:27:14.220 --> 00:27:17.010
Tap that link there,
takes me to the web page.

00:27:17.010 --> 00:27:23.170
I could scroll down, maybe crop
out a piece of that web page

00:27:23.170 --> 00:27:26.221
for marking up and annotating.

00:27:26.221 --> 00:27:30.025
And now I have that on there.

00:27:30.025 --> 00:27:32.120
Just position
these a little bit.

00:27:32.120 --> 00:27:36.681
And so, like I said before,
Hangouts Meet and Jamboard are

00:27:36.681 --> 00:27:37.680
great partners together.

00:27:40.270 --> 00:27:43.730
I can join any Meet
as a participant

00:27:43.730 --> 00:27:45.380
right from the Jamboard.

00:27:45.380 --> 00:27:47.420
So that means I can
share my screen in.

00:27:47.420 --> 00:27:49.520
I can share video
into the Hangout.

00:27:49.520 --> 00:27:51.710
I can really participate
in that Hangout remotely,

00:27:51.710 --> 00:27:53.800
right from the Jamboard,
but more importantly, I

00:27:53.800 --> 00:27:56.480
can share that ideation and that
creation with any other users.

00:27:56.480 --> 00:27:59.679
Obviously these two
can connect as well.

00:27:59.679 --> 00:28:01.970
And it just brings everyone
together onto the same page

00:28:01.970 --> 00:28:05.040
in the same room.

00:28:05.040 --> 00:28:09.170
Lastly, I want to talk a little
bit about G Suite integration.

00:28:09.170 --> 00:28:14.240
I can bring up my Google Docs,
Sheets, and Slides in Drive,

00:28:14.240 --> 00:28:18.790
go into a folder, maybe grab
a document I want to work on,

00:28:18.790 --> 00:28:21.150
put it down there in the corner.

00:28:21.150 --> 00:28:25.250
Then I just double
tap that document,

00:28:25.250 --> 00:28:26.376
and I can drag pages off.

00:28:26.376 --> 00:28:27.750
I'm going to grab
the front page.

00:28:27.750 --> 00:28:30.473
And then let's just get
rid of this piece here.

00:28:33.790 --> 00:28:35.490
And notice I can
just drag stuff down

00:28:35.490 --> 00:28:37.060
to the bottom to get rid of it.

00:28:37.060 --> 00:28:40.260
So I've dragged a
page out of my Doc.

00:28:40.260 --> 00:28:46.330
And I can use that to add to my
Jam session right from my phone

00:28:46.330 --> 00:28:47.200
or from a tablet.

00:28:47.200 --> 00:28:49.420
And we've used
mobile devices quite

00:28:49.420 --> 00:28:53.260
intelligently to act as a
second factor authentication

00:28:53.260 --> 00:28:56.230
to ensure that you
don't accidentally

00:28:56.230 --> 00:28:58.690
leave your Drive
open on a Jam session

00:28:58.690 --> 00:29:00.190
and walk away from the Jamboard.

00:29:00.190 --> 00:29:02.890
So think of that device
as a way of securing

00:29:02.890 --> 00:29:04.620
your content personally to you.

00:29:09.010 --> 00:29:12.920
So I grab my Lasso here.

00:29:12.920 --> 00:29:18.500
And let's just select
everything on the board

00:29:18.500 --> 00:29:21.210
and shrink it down a little bit.

00:29:21.210 --> 00:29:24.670
Because the other factor
I want to point out

00:29:24.670 --> 00:29:29.710
is that all of this stuff
is stored in the Cloud.

00:29:33.500 --> 00:29:34.670
And that means a few things.

00:29:34.670 --> 00:29:39.460
First, it means I get to take
advantage of all the sharing

00:29:39.460 --> 00:29:42.440
built into Google, right?

00:29:42.440 --> 00:29:45.450
I can add people to my Jam,
remove people, and really

00:29:45.450 --> 00:29:47.840
control the access to that.

00:29:47.840 --> 00:29:53.057
It also means I get
autosave every time

00:29:53.057 --> 00:29:54.140
I interact with the board.

00:29:54.140 --> 00:29:59.020
Oh, and you see it got my
messy writing wrong there.

00:29:59.020 --> 00:30:00.190
Rarely does that.

00:30:00.190 --> 00:30:02.740
I can just stroke that out,
rewrite it, or even correct

00:30:02.740 --> 00:30:03.949
individual letters if I want.

00:30:03.949 --> 00:30:05.865
But the point I was
trying to make there was I

00:30:05.865 --> 00:30:07.210
get the autosave functionality.

00:30:07.210 --> 00:30:09.001
Every interaction I
have with the Jamboard,

00:30:09.001 --> 00:30:10.420
automatically
saved to the Cloud.

00:30:10.420 --> 00:30:13.260
I don't have to take a
picture of that anymore.

00:30:13.260 --> 00:30:16.870
I also get unlimited space.

00:30:19.950 --> 00:30:23.070
That means that if I run
out of space on one Jam,

00:30:23.070 --> 00:30:27.060
I can just pop open the top
there, create a new tile,

00:30:27.060 --> 00:30:34.375
maybe drag some content there,
and begin my next session.

00:30:34.375 --> 00:30:35.750
So hopefully you
saw a little bit

00:30:35.750 --> 00:30:38.770
about how the Jamboard
comes together.

00:30:38.770 --> 00:30:41.400
I'll show you on here.

00:30:41.400 --> 00:30:43.020
See how it connects
people, allows

00:30:43.020 --> 00:30:46.050
you to create with
partners in real time,

00:30:46.050 --> 00:30:48.630
and really how it
brings that whiteboard

00:30:48.630 --> 00:30:54.024
into the video conference
digital generation.

00:30:54.024 --> 00:30:56.440
That's really all I wanted to
go over with you guys today,

00:30:56.440 --> 00:30:58.911
but we're going to move
to the Q&amp;A session.

00:30:58.911 --> 00:31:00.660
I know you guys have
been asking questions

00:31:00.660 --> 00:31:03.130
throughout the session.

00:31:03.130 --> 00:31:04.635
So we're going to
take a minute just

00:31:04.635 --> 00:31:06.150
to kind of gather
those up, and then I'll

00:31:06.150 --> 00:31:07.774
be right back to
answer a few of those.

00:33:36.140 --> 00:33:39.096
All right, and we're
back for the Q&amp;A session.

00:33:39.096 --> 00:33:40.720
So I've got a few
questions here coming

00:33:40.720 --> 00:33:43.300
in from my team that have
been looking at your questions

00:33:43.300 --> 00:33:44.360
on the platform.

00:33:44.360 --> 00:33:46.540
Just going to answer them
right from the board here.

00:33:46.540 --> 00:33:48.610
And so first question
I'm going to grab here

00:33:48.610 --> 00:33:51.940
is what if I want to
share my Jam session?

00:33:51.940 --> 00:33:55.480
We've taken advantage of
the sharing mechanisms

00:33:55.480 --> 00:33:56.510
within Google.

00:33:56.510 --> 00:33:58.780
So it uses exact same
sharing paradigms.

00:33:58.780 --> 00:34:00.940
I simply pop open
the side panel.

00:34:00.940 --> 00:34:03.280
I can add people
to the Jam session,

00:34:03.280 --> 00:34:05.200
remove people if I wanted to.

00:34:05.200 --> 00:34:07.750
If I wanted to get rid of
Chris, here, I'd just hit the X.

00:34:07.750 --> 00:34:09.260
And I could add anyone else.

00:34:09.260 --> 00:34:11.650
We also have the ability to
join Jamboard to Jamboard,

00:34:11.650 --> 00:34:13.389
just using this
little short code.

00:34:13.389 --> 00:34:14.739
So that's another way
you could add people,

00:34:14.739 --> 00:34:17.239
in effect, if they were in in
front of a Jamboard like that.

00:34:17.239 --> 00:34:18.120
So great question.

00:34:18.120 --> 00:34:19.540
Thank you.

00:34:19.540 --> 00:34:22.690
Is the Jamboard available
in European markets?

00:34:22.690 --> 00:34:23.590
Yes it is.

00:34:23.590 --> 00:34:28.420
We launched in the UK last year.

00:34:28.420 --> 00:34:34.139
We've recently announced our
launch within Denmark, France,

00:34:34.139 --> 00:34:37.889
the Netherlands,
Spain, and the Nordics,

00:34:37.889 --> 00:34:43.120
so Sweden, Finland, and Norway.

00:34:43.120 --> 00:34:45.530
Thank you.

00:34:45.530 --> 00:34:47.409
So that answers that.

00:34:47.409 --> 00:34:50.550
More to come on that
in the future as well.

00:34:50.550 --> 00:34:53.489
How can Hangouts Meet and
Jamboard work together?

00:34:53.489 --> 00:34:56.400
So Hangouts Meet is
basically accessible right

00:34:56.400 --> 00:34:57.700
from that same side panel.

00:34:57.700 --> 00:34:59.940
So I could connect
into a meeting.

00:34:59.940 --> 00:35:01.820
I just use the 10 digit code.

00:35:01.820 --> 00:35:03.570
And notice there's
actually-- you probably

00:35:03.570 --> 00:35:05.370
can't see it out there, but
there's a little camera here.

00:35:05.370 --> 00:35:06.870
There's a mic built
into the board.

00:35:06.870 --> 00:35:09.300
So I could actually participate
in a Hangouts Meet right

00:35:09.300 --> 00:35:11.370
from the board,
using this camera.

00:35:11.370 --> 00:35:13.170
Or with this little
toggle, I can

00:35:13.170 --> 00:35:15.690
present what's on the
Jamboard into the Meet there.

00:35:15.690 --> 00:35:16.540
Really easy to use.

00:35:16.540 --> 00:35:18.810
Should be familiar to
any Hangouts Meet user

00:35:18.810 --> 00:35:20.060
when they get on the Jamboard.

00:35:22.400 --> 00:35:23.270
Let's take this one.

00:35:23.270 --> 00:35:25.730
Is there a record
and playback feature?

00:35:25.730 --> 00:35:28.100
Very commonly asked
for feature request.

00:35:28.100 --> 00:35:29.600
You know, we're
working on this one,

00:35:29.600 --> 00:35:32.010
and it definitely will
be coming down the road.

00:35:32.010 --> 00:35:33.710
But right now, the
kind of workaround

00:35:33.710 --> 00:35:36.110
is to join a Meet session
and then record that way.

00:35:36.110 --> 00:35:39.700
And you can see how
the ideas came to pass

00:35:39.700 --> 00:35:43.280
or came into creation.

00:35:43.280 --> 00:35:45.360
Let's see here.

00:35:45.360 --> 00:35:47.610
When will we be able
to interact and modify

00:35:47.610 --> 00:35:49.170
Jam on the Chrome browser?

00:35:49.170 --> 00:35:50.779
That is a coming soon as well.

00:35:50.779 --> 00:35:52.320
We're diligently
working on this one.

00:35:52.320 --> 00:35:54.528
This is probably the number
one request we've gotten.

00:35:54.528 --> 00:35:57.480
And you know, there's
some logic behind this.

00:35:57.480 --> 00:36:00.390
We just felt that this
wasn't the way to draw.

00:36:00.390 --> 00:36:02.130
If anybody's tried
to draw with a mouse,

00:36:02.130 --> 00:36:03.930
it is not the type
of user experience

00:36:03.930 --> 00:36:06.790
that really works
out for people.

00:36:06.790 --> 00:36:08.880
So we intentionally
went for things

00:36:08.880 --> 00:36:10.890
that could take
advantage of a stylus,

00:36:10.890 --> 00:36:13.290
or could at least
support better drawing.

00:36:13.290 --> 00:36:15.141
And we felt the
phone and the tablet

00:36:15.141 --> 00:36:16.890
were the right devices
for that, the phone

00:36:16.890 --> 00:36:20.165
for the sticky noting,
and the tablet for finger

00:36:20.165 --> 00:36:21.540
drawing and that's
sort of thing.

00:36:21.540 --> 00:36:24.355
We are going to be releasing
an editor for the browser,

00:36:24.355 --> 00:36:26.730
but we want to make sure we
do that in the right way that

00:36:26.730 --> 00:36:28.500
takes advantage
of the form factor

00:36:28.500 --> 00:36:30.480
that is a laptop or
desktop computer.

00:36:34.820 --> 00:36:37.920
Can the Jamboard app work
without a Jamboard hardware,

00:36:37.920 --> 00:36:38.630
i.e.

00:36:38.630 --> 00:36:40.790
app on an app, tablet to tablet?

00:36:40.790 --> 00:36:42.126
Yes, absolutely.

00:36:42.126 --> 00:36:44.000
We initially thought,
hey, this is just going

00:36:44.000 --> 00:36:45.674
to be a tablet application.

00:36:45.674 --> 00:36:47.340
We built the whole
Jamboard application,

00:36:47.340 --> 00:36:51.260
and every single person was
saying, I wish this was bigger.

00:36:51.260 --> 00:36:52.850
And so it led us to
build the Jamboard

00:36:52.850 --> 00:36:56.030
because we kind of needed that
as the companion to the tablet.

00:36:56.030 --> 00:36:57.650
So this was the first use case.

00:36:57.650 --> 00:36:59.930
It absolutely works tablet
to tablet, phone to phone,

00:36:59.930 --> 00:37:04.710
or however you want
to connect and ideate.

00:37:04.710 --> 00:37:06.870
Are there simple ways to
connect multiple people

00:37:06.870 --> 00:37:08.352
to the Jam at once?

00:37:08.352 --> 00:37:09.810
I kind of showed
this a little bit,

00:37:09.810 --> 00:37:11.220
but I just want to reiterate
a couple of things,

00:37:11.220 --> 00:37:13.720
because there's multiple
ways to connect Jams.

00:37:13.720 --> 00:37:15.790
And I showed a couple here.

00:37:15.790 --> 00:37:19.230
So I have the ability
to join or start

00:37:19.230 --> 00:37:21.780
a broadcast, which means that
I can basically just give

00:37:21.780 --> 00:37:24.314
another Jamboard user a code.

00:37:24.314 --> 00:37:25.980
They can tap that
into their Jam session

00:37:25.980 --> 00:37:27.539
and get that mirroring going.

00:37:27.539 --> 00:37:29.580
I can add people, so
they'll now have the ability

00:37:29.580 --> 00:37:30.840
to jump into the Jam.

00:37:30.840 --> 00:37:32.730
But also right from my
phone, I can actually

00:37:32.730 --> 00:37:35.670
locate nearby boards
and throw my Jam

00:37:35.670 --> 00:37:37.330
onto that board from my phone.

00:37:37.330 --> 00:37:39.420
So just using a
Bluetooth connection,

00:37:39.420 --> 00:37:42.580
I'm able to throw a Jam
that I have onto the board.

00:37:42.580 --> 00:37:45.420
It will just ask for a little
acceptance, and away I go.

00:37:49.410 --> 00:37:52.320
How often does Hangouts
Meet hardware update?

00:37:52.320 --> 00:37:53.779
Is that a manual update?

00:37:53.779 --> 00:37:55.320
It is definitely
not a manual update.

00:37:55.320 --> 00:37:57.250
I do not know the
frequency of the update,

00:37:57.250 --> 00:38:00.690
so I'll have to
follow up on that.

00:38:00.690 --> 00:38:02.239
If somebody on my
answer crew knows,

00:38:02.239 --> 00:38:03.780
they can just throw
a sticky note up.

00:38:03.780 --> 00:38:06.520
But it does update over the air.

00:38:06.520 --> 00:38:09.290
So we push out the updates
to the devices themselves.

00:38:09.290 --> 00:38:12.030
And one of the really powerful
elements of that is it also

00:38:12.030 --> 00:38:14.560
includes qualified peripherals.

00:38:14.560 --> 00:38:17.162
So if your Speakermic needs
updated with firmware,

00:38:17.162 --> 00:38:18.870
we can push that right
into the Chromebox

00:38:18.870 --> 00:38:22.114
and right through the wire
to that Speakermic, meaning

00:38:22.114 --> 00:38:23.780
you don't have to
take care of anything.

00:38:23.780 --> 00:38:27.270
You just let that
work on its own.

00:38:27.270 --> 00:38:31.140
Am I able to use any of the
existing equipment with HMH?

00:38:31.140 --> 00:38:33.370
So this is kind of a
case by case basis.

00:38:33.370 --> 00:38:37.380
It's hard to kind of give an
overall, overarching answer.

00:38:37.380 --> 00:38:40.350
We would recommend using
it with the equipment

00:38:40.350 --> 00:38:42.690
that it comes with,
but we found that a lot

00:38:42.690 --> 00:38:46.530
of speakers and mics
and even cameras

00:38:46.530 --> 00:38:47.734
will work with the kits.

00:38:47.734 --> 00:38:49.650
You just kind of have
to try that out yourself

00:38:49.650 --> 00:38:50.483
and see if it works.

00:38:50.483 --> 00:38:53.650
But in many cases, you're able
to swap in different devices.

00:38:53.650 --> 00:38:55.320
And we are working
diligently to expand

00:38:55.320 --> 00:38:57.240
that qualified
peripherals program

00:38:57.240 --> 00:39:01.210
to include and encompass
more options that way.

00:39:01.210 --> 00:39:05.234
Last one, is there a demo
or trial period for HMH?

00:39:05.234 --> 00:39:07.150
Well, there's a couple
things I'd say to this.

00:39:07.150 --> 00:39:10.330
We don't have an official
trial or demo program at Google

00:39:10.330 --> 00:39:11.387
at this time.

00:39:11.387 --> 00:39:12.970
It's something we've
discussed and are

00:39:12.970 --> 00:39:14.535
working on and thinking about.

00:39:14.535 --> 00:39:16.660
But definitely reach out
to your account executive,

00:39:16.660 --> 00:39:19.240
and we can discuss
what the options are.

00:39:19.240 --> 00:39:22.510
But the nice thing about
HMH is you don't actually

00:39:22.510 --> 00:39:25.780
need anything more than a
browser or a mobile device

00:39:25.780 --> 00:39:27.310
to try out the experience.

00:39:27.310 --> 00:39:30.010
So you can try this
without all the hardware,

00:39:30.010 --> 00:39:33.190
see if it works for you, sort
of based on the functionality,

00:39:33.190 --> 00:39:34.611
and then decide
if the hardware is

00:39:34.611 --> 00:39:35.860
a direction you want to go in.

00:39:39.380 --> 00:39:41.180
So that's it for the questions.

00:39:41.180 --> 00:39:43.730
Really appreciate everybody
taking the time today

00:39:43.730 --> 00:39:45.210
to join us.

00:39:45.210 --> 00:39:47.780
Thank you for tuning
in to Cloud OnAir.

00:39:47.780 --> 00:39:50.635
Please remember, we're hosting
these live every Tuesday.

00:39:50.635 --> 00:39:52.010
Hopefully you can
join next week.

00:39:52.010 --> 00:39:53.590
Like I said, check the agenda.

00:39:53.590 --> 00:39:55.340
There's a whole bunch
of different topics.

00:39:55.340 --> 00:39:57.506
You'll definitely find
something of interest to you.

00:39:57.506 --> 00:39:59.530
Thanks again, and take care.

